            "Inspired by the idea of comparing Lao-tzu's ideology with Machiavelli's point of view in the 7th suggestion for critical writing on page 217, I will focus on analyzing the difference in political theories of Lao-tzu and Machiavelli by explicating their ideas concerning the qualities one needs in order to be a good ruler. Moverover, I want to build my own ideal model of a modern leader by combining both Lao Tzu and Machiavelli's political views together.\nFirstly, I will highlight the difference between Lao-tzu's Master and Machiavelli's prince by dividing this point into three sections: opposing perspectives in terms of war, contradictory views on the matter of morality, and the matters of the question if a great leader should be loved or feared. To support my analysis, I will use quotations from two main texts, The Prince by Niccolò Machiavelli and Tao-te Ching by Lao-tzu. On the question of war, I will show that while Lao Tzu believed that war and weapons were unnecessary, Machiavelli felt that a leader's first concern should be war. As regards to morality, Lao-tzu's belief is that people will ultimately be moral if there are fewer rules; Machiavelli, on the other hand, affirmed that people need laws and a fear of punishment to force them into being moral. For the last section of this main point, I will present Lao Tzu's preference for a beloved leader and Machiavelli's assertion of the need for a feared leader.\nSecondly, I will come up with my own version of a good leader, also based on the three issues in the first main point, and provide my arguments to prove my thoughts. In my opinion, it is unnecessary for a leader to set war as the first concern in peacetime, but a wise leader must always have a plan ready to deal with the unexpected war. Moreover, one of the key things to being a good leader, in my point of view, is knowing how to use just enough laws, rules and regulations to be able to maintain social order but still ensure human freedom. Furthermore, a great leader should be loved but also feared when needed.\nIn the conclusion of the essay, I will emphasize my idea of a leader who wisely and skillfully selects and renews the political ideas of the past to suit modern life.",
